# Workspace Policy

Summary: Non-overridable COMMON rules for every project and agent in this workspace.

## Rule strength

- **MUST** rules are invariants. No project context, constraint or decision may weaken or override them; projects may only strengthen them.
- **DEFAULT** rules are inherited unless a project explicitly replaces them, normally through an accepted decision.

Every rule in this file is a MUST.

## Repository boundary

- P-01 Private AI context lives only under `.agents/context/` in this private meta-repository.
- P-02 Top-level project directories registered in `.gitmodules` are independent project repositories. Registration does not determine their visibility; private context never goes inside them.
- P-03 Requirement artifacts in a project repository stand on their own: they never reference or depend on `.agents/context/`.
- P-04 A project repository remains understandable, buildable and testable without this meta-repository.
- P-05 `.gitignore` is workspace hygiene only. Confidentiality depends on repository topology and review, never on ignore rules.
- P-06 `.worktrees/**` and `.agents/runtime/**` are never tracked by the meta-repository.
- P-07 Top-level projects are true Git submodules (gitlinks), never absorbed directories.

## Secrets

- P-08 Secret values, credentials, private keys, tokens and passwords are never committed anywhere, in context, issues or project files.
- P-09 Private context may record secret names, environment-variable names and retrieval procedures, never values.

## Context levels

- P-10 COMMON context is the set of direct files under `.agents/context/`, owned by the workspace maintainers. PROJECT context is `.agents/context/<id>/`. GLOBAL preferences are external to the workspace: optional, owned by the user's coding tool, and never discovered, provisioned or modified by `za` or by agents. Personal preferences are defaults; COMMON boundaries, project formatter settings and CI apply to the work they govern.
- P-11 Work with one active project unless the task is explicitly multi-project. A multi-project task processes project contexts sequentially and never assumes one project's defaults apply to the next.
- P-12 A project context is understandable from COMMON, that project's private context, that project's repository and its declared authoritative references alone. It never requires, and is never authoritative for, another project.
- P-13 When switching projects, retain only COMMON context and load the new project from zero.

## Execution state

- P-14 Task worktrees under `.worktrees/<id>/<task-id>/` are execution state, not context and not additional projects. Inspect only the active one; never scan `.worktrees/` recursively.
- P-15 Normal writable work happens in a task worktree created from the pinned gitlink or an explicitly verified revision, not in the canonical checkout.
- P-16 A normal write task stops when upstream freshness or context verification is stale or pending. When freshness cannot be established, say so; never claim it.
- P-17 Remove task worktrees through Git (`git worktree remove`, then `git worktree prune`). Never run destructive `git clean` over `.worktrees/`.

## Authority

- P-18 Every requirement scope has at most one declared authority, recorded in the project's `sot.md`; a scope needed for implementation must have exactly one. Physical location, format and recency never confer authority by themselves.
- P-19 Unresolved requirement authority that affects implementation is surfaced before that implementation proceeds, never guessed around.
- P-20 Observable behavior: the declared authority for the scope (an assigned OpenSpec change, current OpenSpec spec, another tracker, or a privately authoritative specification) > implementation > private notes. A detected specification is a candidate until the owning review confirms it.
- P-21 Technical architecture: global MUST > accepted project decision > global DEFAULT. A decision never overrides a MUST and never redeclares observable behavior; behavior authority changes go through `sot.md`.
- P-22 Dependencies: project dependency files > private workspace overlays.
- P-23 `status.md` is informational only and never overrides specifications, rules or issue records.
- P-24 When code and the declared requirement disagree, determine which is stale and repair that source of truth. Never silently normalize the requirement to the code.
